BODY={151:'The complete cases preserve fishing actions around the couplet: hanging line, hook, deep water, hooked fish, and Jiashan being pressed to speak.',152:'The repeated gesture is not interchangeable with the bead count: the chief seat and Dadian each perform three audible tooth-clicks in the same inquiry.',153:'The retained clauses explicitly rank the criticized saying or realization as second; they do not name a geographical or temporal “head.”',154:'The witnesses connect the embryo with continued nourishment, maturation, and secluded residence after an initial attainment, preserving the gestational image.',155:'Both clauses remain joined across direct answers, verse-like appraisals, and comments on earlier cases: the bodily sign supplies the reason for refusing association.',156:'The witnesses retain both hard actions—knocking bone and beating marrow—inside clauses of exacting, forceful treatment; no literal injury is narrated.',157:'Some cases praise reliable hitting, while others explicitly say the same perfect record remains inadequate or costly; the arrow-score image itself stays unchanged.',158:'The two clauses compare circular leaf to mirror and pointed caltrop to awl; the following willow-floss and pear-blossom lines are a related continuation, not part of this headword.',159:'The verb phrase specifies bad water, sudden direct impact, the head, and pouring; its targets range from interlocutors to the Buddha-bathing event.',160:'The horse-colt epithet and trampling predicate recur together in verse and appraisal; the surrounding cases identify Mazu rather than an unnamed literal animal.'}
NOTE={151:'The fishing imagery is explicit in the surrounding hook, line, pool, wave, and fish vocabulary; “intent” belongs to the spoken couplet and is not an inferred motive.',152:'The first click in several transmitted versions belongs to the unnamed chief seat, not Dadian; both later make the same gesture, so each retained span is actor-checked.',153:'“Second” is a stated ranking in these appraisals. The entry supplies no account of an unstated first position.',154:'The records themselves use gestation language of nurturing and maturation. This entry reports that imagery without treating it as physiology.',155:'The phrase is a warning formula, not evidence that the appraised person literally has an abnormal face.',156:'The paired anatomy is figurative in these sermons and instructions; no retained case reports bones or marrow being physically struck.',157:'Because speakers both commend and depreciate perfect hitting, the idiom alone records accuracy and does not guarantee approval.',158:'The couplet states ordinary visible resemblances. Later discussions call it a “resembling phrase,” but the images receive no hidden symbolic gloss.',159:'The foul-water wording is an insult or adverse appraisal in these cases; it is not a sanitation description.',160:'“Young horse” is the traditional epithet invoked for Mazu in the retained Chan passages; the trampling remains appraisal language, not a death report.'}
ALIAS={151:'The lookup spells out the thousand-foot line and its stated aim in the deep pool, preserving both halves of the fishing couplet.',152:'The lookup keeps the performed tooth-click, the count of three, and the repeated gesture rather than the nearby bead count of one hundred and eight.',153:'The lookup retains the verb “fall,” the ranked “second,” and 頭 as position; “second position” alone would omit the adverse movement.',154:'The lookup preserves both feeding or nurturing and the embryo image; “sacred embryo” alone would lose the continuing action.',155:'The lookup keeps the visible cheeks as the condition and refusal of association as its consequence.',156:'The lookup names the two coordinated actions and their distinct anatomical objects: knocking bones and beating marrow.',157:'The lookup preserves the numerical completeness of both attempts and hits; “perfect accuracy” is a useful gloss but not the literal arrow-count formula.',158:'The lookup retains both plant shapes, both repeated adjectives, and their separate mirror and awl comparisons.',159:'The lookup keeps foul water, abrupt head-on direction, the head, and pouring; “drenching” alone loses the hostile material and direction.',160:'The lookup identifies the colt, the fatal trampling predicate, and its world-wide object; “Mazu prevails” would erase the recorded animal image.'}
FAMILY={151:'Occurrences containing only a long line, a deep pool, or fishing intent are excluded; the retained expression states all three together.',152:'A single tooth click or an unspecified clicking gesture is not counted because the records specify three clicks.',153:'Other references to a second item or second head lack the predicate of falling into that ranked position.',154:'Embryo language without the verb for continued nurturing does not attest this action-image.',155:'Cheeks behind the head without the accompanying refusal, or a refusal without the bodily sign, does not preserve the warning pair.',156:'Bone or marrow imagery by itself does not reproduce the paired sequence of knocking and beating.',157:'Archery, hitting, or repeated success without the matched hundred attempts and hundred hits does not attest the fixed idiom.',158:'The willow-floss and pear-blossom continuation is excluded: it lacks the lotus-leaf mirror and caltrop-awl comparisons that define this couplet.',159:'Other water pouring and other insults are excluded unless foul water is poured abruptly onto the head.',160:'Other horse and trampling lines are excluded unless the colt tramples all people under heaven.'}
